[{:db/id "q-102328/selection/4243e6462c01a0f8c7929e248821c0cb16d103501f063bc34b2e3fe7fa55bb10" :answer/id #uuid "e7cab519-a103-5625-874a-77b49134560e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102328/selection/fc1c6668388e636d2da72c2573ab36b9cda9d313fb248be08ac19ddff2721451" :answer/id #uuid "ce08e2c3-f8c8-529e-a272-e965219ef45a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(3,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102328/selection/4118bc58c734397ed6279f4651e412efc9d9063789f27921dfdcc1f70042efb2" :answer/id #uuid "4107d8ff-393c-588d-855e-f98929daa1df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102328/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" :answer/id #uuid "45aabc16-1f22-5c5c-8b94-974d01985d24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102328/selection/890e136204e0f4dd706dc84f66dc8bc21ab96a78f0b6339394e8ce1700472cc2" :answer/id #uuid "75c146fc-c19c-5dbd-952f-67cd4eb2e410" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102328/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b6e59c8-6bfb-535c-9de6-19e6f89eaff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-102328/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" :answer-field/choices ["q-102328/selection/4243e6462c01a0f8c7929e248821c0cb16d103501f063bc34b2e3fe7fa55bb10" "q-102328/selection/fc1c6668388e636d2da72c2573ab36b9cda9d313fb248be08ac19ddff2721451" "q-102328/selection/4118bc58c734397ed6279f4651e412efc9d9063789f27921dfdcc1f70042efb2" "q-102328/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" "q-102328/selection/890e136204e0f4dd706dc84f66dc8bc21ab96a78f0b6339394e8ce1700472cc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-102328" :question/id #uuid "493c2503-d927-5c33-a8cb-65c2e910cf8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-102328" :question/problem "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x-2y+12.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6 & 2y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6=0 \\\\ 2y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-102328/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-102328"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925/selection/83bce766dc110aa3f6fcef3cf2b8103b42c4cd000a0a8cc4d2da1d9fab9a401c" :answer/id #uuid "e44238b9-bf24-5223-b153-88832c918613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6,-9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925/selection/b19656ca36e33e2e9a525e708393c66664aca5e4b57507350d24747ae712a978" :answer/id #uuid "429cf398-eb87-536b-9bdb-354ba539802e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925/selection/bbfd2b07786245819e2e91f26a4ef8e027e05def7f6b7b047ffd4f9538889061" :answer/id #uuid "dca5c2ea-c4f7-5266-b3cc-831ac8ea2527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925/selection/0058a3d0ccdaa0489c6c6683df26e9525c21f6e4c3654b39c56725ee10bd5d81" :answer/id #uuid "e3b73635-070a-5893-bb2d-84a8f01d3b63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7" :answer/id #uuid "21960eef-4077-505e-875f-2df261e215b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6aee779d-08e4-572f-bf5c-a219f164a7d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-103925/selection/bbfd2b07786245819e2e91f26a4ef8e027e05def7f6b7b047ffd4f9538889061" :answer-field/choices ["q-103925/selection/83bce766dc110aa3f6fcef3cf2b8103b42c4cd000a0a8cc4d2da1d9fab9a401c" "q-103925/selection/b19656ca36e33e2e9a525e708393c66664aca5e4b57507350d24747ae712a978" "q-103925/selection/bbfd2b07786245819e2e91f26a4ef8e027e05def7f6b7b047ffd4f9538889061" "q-103925/selection/0058a3d0ccdaa0489c6c6683df26e9525c21f6e4c3654b39c56725ee10bd5d81" "q-103925/selection/0af8632f8d775c0447fbe2b8d1621b1c19cbb186090770a99ba5bb47342490c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103925" :question/id #uuid "ba793541-bf07-5369-9d6f-63098b7e3fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103925" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+xy-3x+3y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y+x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3 & 2y+x+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3=0 \\\\ 2y+x+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=3-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3-2x)+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 6-4x+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 9-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $y=3-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3-2(3)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,-3).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103925/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103925"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36622"] :question/problem "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2xy+{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-2y=0 \\\\ -2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{(-1)}=-1 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$\nWe reject the point $(-1,-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646/selection/2d6f10c56c19b75914a3ae31f75d2f7ba34871a88d00d9c231324339795e64b6" :answer/id #uuid "868b8a28-e0d2-5596-b16d-1936b3fb0767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646/selection/487b4f0bc631c70ccd022e8f80d7a40d76d660baab96311cba73301d6feff0a9" :answer/id #uuid "214a1134-cd31-5d9f-bc61-e01bae2e7b0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646/selection/d575978d84535aab2cd1c2f37b6246e6a631d0d59223c991c39b4148e17ef47d" :answer/id #uuid "c072f670-4486-5e92-ac0c-8f3170c8b1ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646/selection/5fe19a0cac2cad25e6be264df48c29dac0c869f4523734ce4c6c70eb10601415" :answer/id #uuid "2987c67a-c6ff-53bf-9300-fbdda6968919" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646/selection/57a551d180a2cd752ea4dad82d0d5e20dd7c836abe02c03727fe0f86dafcd3ab" :answer/id #uuid "5e8ddad8-3775-5a1c-8240-4f0802adbff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "afe74ba8-78a7-570b-bd79-1be4d2c275d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80646/selection/57a551d180a2cd752ea4dad82d0d5e20dd7c836abe02c03727fe0f86dafcd3ab" :answer-field/choices ["q-80646/selection/2d6f10c56c19b75914a3ae31f75d2f7ba34871a88d00d9c231324339795e64b6" "q-80646/selection/487b4f0bc631c70ccd022e8f80d7a40d76d660baab96311cba73301d6feff0a9" "q-80646/selection/d575978d84535aab2cd1c2f37b6246e6a631d0d59223c991c39b4148e17ef47d" "q-80646/selection/5fe19a0cac2cad25e6be264df48c29dac0c869f4523734ce4c6c70eb10601415" "q-80646/selection/57a551d180a2cd752ea4dad82d0d5e20dd7c836abe02c03727fe0f86dafcd3ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80646" :question/id #uuid "37b606fb-2183-5f5c-9520-4ea005b108eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80646" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4xy+{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =0 \\\\ -4x+(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(\\frac{1}{2})}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ We reject the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685/selection/dee1317613a15ec552a7e2abff7785436ebf9695e17660ec443e77534f75131a" :answer/id #uuid "2ff96934-d06c-5531-a9b1-ee1ea4dd8f14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685/selection/3809cd979cc5da34b91fc9dfe2ac7163c585222627547a14bf8877e26cc40113" :answer/id #uuid "5af0e9ea-0b8f-514a-a763-9f121d875e20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685/selection/3bedbfbfb1f98903ec94e5e84918e6b63ddc286dedf897c192e351b991e10135" :answer/id #uuid "0059affa-2596-5d22-ba99-f5900753fcf7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685/selection/565a5672d7a54f758531d3a18df05ba6f5360e85e49636c07cae4b021d14c7f9" :answer/id #uuid "7fe877e7-1722-5cae-8d3c-20f78a12752a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685/selection/11a50460ffc4fc0f50b381a9431c8d695068483aab79968c30edabb5bfda1258" :answer/id #uuid "5d85a239-eb69-5624-89e0-de2b205949b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6be81859-d6d8-5f72-9c2c-c34dcd30938a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80685/selection/3809cd979cc5da34b91fc9dfe2ac7163c585222627547a14bf8877e26cc40113" :answer-field/choices ["q-80685/selection/dee1317613a15ec552a7e2abff7785436ebf9695e17660ec443e77534f75131a" "q-80685/selection/3809cd979cc5da34b91fc9dfe2ac7163c585222627547a14bf8877e26cc40113" "q-80685/selection/3bedbfbfb1f98903ec94e5e84918e6b63ddc286dedf897c192e351b991e10135" "q-80685/selection/565a5672d7a54f758531d3a18df05ba6f5360e85e49636c07cae4b021d14c7f9" "q-80685/selection/11a50460ffc4fc0f50b381a9431c8d695068483aab79968c30edabb5bfda1258"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80685" :question/id #uuid "61d61eae-7f7f-53de-97ab-4c73ee838831" :question/math-academy-id "q-80685" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+6{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80685/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80685"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "d7318c30-73d4-50b5-aa50-1c5747130ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "64b474ec-bb74-52e5-ac75-498b70c8f29a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "cc3e04f5-42fc-5738-b049-fcbf23e9c6a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "88c387fe-9eaf-57ea-b9da-66b92be4c6c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8757c4ef-bd5c-5447-bc0b-2b9d8fd398ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "edca972a-c43e-55a0-84fa-4d1fba93a955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104995/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-104995/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-104995/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-104995/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-104995/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-104995/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104995" :question/id #uuid "6af8edba-09e4-5b04-83ba-ff9e01fbc281" :question/math-academy-id "q-104995" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104995/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104995"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "dbdba9cb-8d99-5e71-b72f-5aa8ee177eb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "545b6df1-e7c9-5b96-be9b-3047d65b88b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "e9f3a5a8-4ed0-5fb8-85b2-0199c4929238" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fbe918d3-f2d2-5b15-9537-25c15e291123" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "a8405eef-0023-5f69-9a13-22ff7fd2e4e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea924284-0cc4-51ff-a22c-69785c5b5b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104984/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-104984/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-104984/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-104984/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-104984/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-104984/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104984" :question/id #uuid "8e4002c3-ae8f-50f5-b36b-347ebe1b0f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-104984" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104984/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104984"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "cf5656a4-a613-5331-8fe8-c67dfdf7bdd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "4a3a6421-f2d3-58d7-9cc2-a1286cb3e99a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b7ad5206-6c7d-5ccf-9fbe-9196224992b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "602adfd2-4490-59e5-a8fb-a759398eac50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "7b40a2ea-486c-5467-9387-5a9c64c59ce9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a8ef7df-4c8f-5a39-85be-bf895e8f3f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81164/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-81164/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-81164/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-81164/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-81164/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-81164/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81164" :question/id #uuid "6879580a-ed3c-5479-82f5-d8d6e4ea2d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-81164" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81164/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81164"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37588"] :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7c98a72f-ab26-53d6-a5ed-fe1c17833a6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a55bcecb-a983-5e1d-b3eb-df6f2b4d4e30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c1ffd4d7-5dee-59ef-9b99-5c34684f7242" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6d612087-0e03-5e0d-b954-0c8225f8fe86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ac4388be-f3b7-53a0-b4aa-c26ef14e24dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4a61c8-b539-5756-8f15-590f50bf4ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37449/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-37449/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-37449/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-37449/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-37449/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-37449/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37449" :question/id #uuid "2cda54b3-c99d-5bf7-829f-6333b30e42aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-37449" :question/problem "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(4,8)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$\n- The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37449/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37449"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043/selection/33e3a3fb53a8f812a84535811e2b7fcc8d50ffabcfc11ae5a86ef9ca91cc54e5" :answer/id #uuid "4f754cf3-a0b6-51f8-8e46-bc8c19c0b418" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043/selection/a02e2eda5d16f44b4db759aa24e82132fffa85036303856c5884a07210afe9bb" :answer/id #uuid "76bedb92-371c-545b-be54-437de68cbc21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043/selection/48d93ec5458faaadab645cdc1ec6ffe2be43a249c48d942811d629019a8f925e" :answer/id #uuid "5da179a1-fb2a-5322-ba79-f4a4519548a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043/selection/4dee80371f91ee95374dd123d757f47dc023ef92ba6308aa36d44b07395c0501" :answer/id #uuid "44d840cc-1be2-5890-9dd2-7dca3a7f5c39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043/selection/74eeed4e67adf1e254f5a64c23d25377745715bc4ccd5174a3b268b54c3c7e7a" :answer/id #uuid "b4599e1c-98c3-5d3a-b58b-cf1d8bab3f16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8c87ff-6cc8-534f-bce4-4dd6bc118911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81043/selection/74eeed4e67adf1e254f5a64c23d25377745715bc4ccd5174a3b268b54c3c7e7a" :answer-field/choices ["q-81043/selection/33e3a3fb53a8f812a84535811e2b7fcc8d50ffabcfc11ae5a86ef9ca91cc54e5" "q-81043/selection/a02e2eda5d16f44b4db759aa24e82132fffa85036303856c5884a07210afe9bb" "q-81043/selection/48d93ec5458faaadab645cdc1ec6ffe2be43a249c48d942811d629019a8f925e" "q-81043/selection/4dee80371f91ee95374dd123d757f47dc023ef92ba6308aa36d44b07395c0501" "q-81043/selection/74eeed4e67adf1e254f5a64c23d25377745715bc4ccd5174a3b268b54c3c7e7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81043" :question/id #uuid "d6d8bf42-1a66-5e36-832b-b1d8f827ac5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81043" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1 & 2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1=0 \\\\ 2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-1)={e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=1\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=2>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-81043/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81043"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281/selection/02c906876cf9f5df70d55bbee8527c4ea95313da1164d21fe1bdc3012351dadd" :answer/id #uuid "a84def85-480b-5b20-aab4-9756748ec57b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281/selection/84880ef031c7f1a23f72c3a8b4a45ee5e975e0286039581a8fe65ef0ad987ac7" :answer/id #uuid "536b69a1-c752-5b37-b38a-f95603ac39fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281/selection/8b93519cd08475c40ab406525e64de2f4b6f9bc6a21af0673018f698ff5d3b0f" :answer/id #uuid "94fbbe7d-ca82-521c-a9e7-3352c959a9af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281/selection/638b7f4a1710b3bc23333a1cc7eeb7276185d5c779223edb6967ff6bc8e3f5c1" :answer/id #uuid "1eae0aba-19a0-5c14-9cc2-e6715cbccb0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281/selection/7bd6d0e3c2961f6dc23c15a72373f546dfcf9fd6dff06918fc64145493a10f72" :answer/id #uuid "035244ea-94da-5459-86da-9dc03ce65543" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c8d579d-8b0c-51aa-a4bb-9cc72e478714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105281/selection/02c906876cf9f5df70d55bbee8527c4ea95313da1164d21fe1bdc3012351dadd" :answer-field/choices ["q-105281/selection/02c906876cf9f5df70d55bbee8527c4ea95313da1164d21fe1bdc3012351dadd" "q-105281/selection/84880ef031c7f1a23f72c3a8b4a45ee5e975e0286039581a8fe65ef0ad987ac7" "q-105281/selection/8b93519cd08475c40ab406525e64de2f4b6f9bc6a21af0673018f698ff5d3b0f" "q-105281/selection/638b7f4a1710b3bc23333a1cc7eeb7276185d5c779223edb6967ff6bc8e3f5c1" "q-105281/selection/7bd6d0e3c2961f6dc23c15a72373f546dfcf9fd6dff06918fc64145493a10f72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105281" :question/id #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105281" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105281/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105281"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167/selection/48d93ec5458faaadab645cdc1ec6ffe2be43a249c48d942811d629019a8f925e" :answer/id #uuid "0c8539ec-ed32-5264-82db-9702a83a9474" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167/selection/74eeed4e67adf1e254f5a64c23d25377745715bc4ccd5174a3b268b54c3c7e7a" :answer/id #uuid "d5860881-057c-50d6-b988-858dcab68836" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167/selection/2c141900cfbf00aeea33095cd038fc2d9e6c4038227d2f8fec72fbb3be303c94" :answer/id #uuid "fe283b24-dc9f-5f7f-b7f6-e2ba52d2b996" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167/selection/5ccfa8c025b6804cdb6476aa2d2e015f6d05602580ec63cee6a5ac5828fadf28" :answer/id #uuid "daaac059-edff-5659-a50f-66915107742a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167/selection/8454a2adf34cc6288c275e107f19f6c6a95f5b8fe4a115ecb15ca52db5abcbd0" :answer/id #uuid "e4035e04-cbcd-5aa4-a0f9-8ddc827bb3bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "38ce8d3a-1d12-54db-af78-d4b96622d008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81167/selection/74eeed4e67adf1e254f5a64c23d25377745715bc4ccd5174a3b268b54c3c7e7a" :answer-field/choices ["q-81167/selection/48d93ec5458faaadab645cdc1ec6ffe2be43a249c48d942811d629019a8f925e" "q-81167/selection/74eeed4e67adf1e254f5a64c23d25377745715bc4ccd5174a3b268b54c3c7e7a" "q-81167/selection/2c141900cfbf00aeea33095cd038fc2d9e6c4038227d2f8fec72fbb3be303c94" "q-81167/selection/5ccfa8c025b6804cdb6476aa2d2e015f6d05602580ec63cee6a5ac5828fadf28" "q-81167/selection/8454a2adf34cc6288c275e107f19f6c6a95f5b8fe4a115ecb15ca52db5abcbd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81167" :question/id #uuid "e1be0ef8-da54-5ba5-b724-bceca43b6b0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81167" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-81167/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81167"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338/selection/9bf231dd921563074d5d9dd60580ac29c9e32a2ce57f17d491a09945b4605b7b" :answer/id #uuid "76bc33f7-306d-5c7f-ab67-ca4670fc50e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338/selection/2ed4cde8e60adcf41250b6cafb8a23629e1a7b8936c65f1ab43082ebc5fb0a18" :answer/id #uuid "bf464b6d-fd9f-5ab4-a32a-711279992db2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(e,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338/selection/c56240a568cb840c5f3cef8f3f8f9f1433e8f43d3272076d9a10c0a90ff7c26c" :answer/id #uuid "437e6a8b-d401-5b8c-aad5-30c12faa41c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(e,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338/selection/a02e2eda5d16f44b4db759aa24e82132fffa85036303856c5884a07210afe9bb" :answer/id #uuid "0a1323e6-f65b-59fc-b9a2-a47b0c32e4cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338/selection/8ff98fc9168add0c39423f6f7dfa7d46b4ff9d819062e91d8671b3e4b59cf9fc" :answer/id #uuid "8d9897fc-2991-52fc-80be-c3f8fb5a59a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "33795d29-fffc-526d-af18-f9bdc9afde14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105338/selection/8ff98fc9168add0c39423f6f7dfa7d46b4ff9d819062e91d8671b3e4b59cf9fc" :answer-field/choices ["q-105338/selection/9bf231dd921563074d5d9dd60580ac29c9e32a2ce57f17d491a09945b4605b7b" "q-105338/selection/2ed4cde8e60adcf41250b6cafb8a23629e1a7b8936c65f1ab43082ebc5fb0a18" "q-105338/selection/c56240a568cb840c5f3cef8f3f8f9f1433e8f43d3272076d9a10c0a90ff7c26c" "q-105338/selection/a02e2eda5d16f44b4db759aa24e82132fffa85036303856c5884a07210afe9bb" "q-105338/selection/8ff98fc9168add0c39423f6f7dfa7d46b4ff9d819062e91d8671b3e4b59cf9fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105338" :question/id #uuid "e1b5f6e8-a9c9-5be3-86f7-77f082bfcf04" :question/math-academy-id "q-105338" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2(0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=0\\cdot (-2)-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-105338/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105338"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335/selection/5cf42c81d6f6958675f00e4cb98c39d029dec417f664cdf61d5f9ff2e0edbbe8" :answer/id #uuid "1b8a5f35-1c6d-50e4-8dea-e977640de21a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local minimum at $(3,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335/selection/a7d23d4ad6ca89f112d968f3f92a16a57ff82d1b78498d0c27a0d38711daccdd" :answer/id #uuid "6c445d67-76bb-5dae-ae9f-67688f05a4e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local maximum at $(3,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335/selection/667a4a4c26424bc1d55071faad4be8674db69d8dc8bad3368798f28b4c05fc8b" :answer/id #uuid "d080fb84-c693-56ab-ae1f-733d9ab8fdba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335/selection/d2a1fe0d3212f72402721cc7b06807c10009f77c532b521ccf1f49ccc3b991d7" :answer/id #uuid "1df45088-53cf-5f17-a1b1-dfe6e27bf52c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335/selection/c52572b103d6a23798deafa20077e3692083ba2f2900e196f0bbb602be991f4f" :answer/id #uuid "56b80100-2bcc-5d96-945d-61f98b6f8616" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(5,-4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e2de5b2-b8fd-59e2-ba02-7325ef5c6d46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105335/selection/d2a1fe0d3212f72402721cc7b06807c10009f77c532b521ccf1f49ccc3b991d7" :answer-field/choices ["q-105335/selection/5cf42c81d6f6958675f00e4cb98c39d029dec417f664cdf61d5f9ff2e0edbbe8" "q-105335/selection/a7d23d4ad6ca89f112d968f3f92a16a57ff82d1b78498d0c27a0d38711daccdd" "q-105335/selection/667a4a4c26424bc1d55071faad4be8674db69d8dc8bad3368798f28b4c05fc8b" "q-105335/selection/d2a1fe0d3212f72402721cc7b06807c10009f77c532b521ccf1f49ccc3b991d7" "q-105335/selection/c52572b103d6a23798deafa20077e3692083ba2f2900e196f0bbb602be991f4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105335" :question/id #uuid "464c724b-219c-5ba2-83f5-2198f7162e1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105335" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=(-4)\\cdot 0-(-4)^{2}=-16<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(5,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(5,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(5,-4)=det({f}^{″}(5,-4))=(-4)\\cdot (-8)-(-4)^{2}=16>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(5,-4)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(5,-4)={h}_{11}=-4<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105335/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105335"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12584"] :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/db60be51a74c53caf3495a85e960be92ca8173c24c168e0ba30e335ef163be9e.png)\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy=3x(x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/2faec0235882f18aa523890be6f7f52f446219ba9a6c27fe8f8179ae90050d0d.png)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$\n\nSetting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x(x-2y)=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y.$ Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3,\n$$\n\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n\n- For $x = 2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12585"] :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y) \n%= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/083732de2321f70e27269ba55cec2a744a9424d443f68b4948caf313d30ae630.png)\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,-1) > 0$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) <    0$\n\n- The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/d680921af1b938e22a2705516c9f463cbb8b7bdb3ab1646b4613f6191e2a06f2.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12586"] :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/47070dbaf25d08b50c3f42c5f4ef9ce7d02b4e541112fa3049126f2d5cdcffed.png) \ngiven that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/7e7f250d8444de28d127551ad1024b9b829d8ce8851cdb644efc69ad9469ce6d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/849819e826aeb3943d8aa6e489d270d71b85ed63f77c50f1b84ab0b93a399e1e.png) occurs at its critical points. A point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/e604f9fa7e2d6f44f89ab4e5d557b403fdcd86b7203502d0d7a71d0f4085a43d.png) in the domain of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/483e14a283d6074afea743287da4d8a82af7d6fb44d37fad07e656580dd3d808.png) is a critical point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/8538defb8f14b78578b1e76a85947d6f73821c3c6bd13c8e12c22925b2459574.png) if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/0ea5054ca54fa11a9c5a0f9d9bb62d1687ab226d9c28ae4f165515cd65bcbccc.png) (for stationary points) or ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/f49532342fc531783f16cecbbf3ec4de8d8344339289530641fe944998ccefdf.png) doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/cbf525e2f109ea8dde911a201ee6a5b426360c104882c0933ae26d5118526c4e.png) is a stationary point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/3fcea4fdc10480ae805cb6e4b6f12da8537c61155dad9ab5bc820e3d99ff3486.png) with Hessian determinant ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/6a1bac6bd44817bbb9e795f522d9a2506750106f8a5f81cda56bd9248e2d4eee.png) we have the following:\n\n- If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/4b418e57d2fb8052581d21dac74f53c18726838ebd601bc8c579003dd8e4